<section class="block" id="work" aria-labelledby="work-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2 id="work-title">Selected work</h2>
    <p class="work-intro">Two products I directed, designed and built end to end.</p>

    <article class="case" id="cardstock" aria-labelledby="cs-title">
      <div class="case-head">
        <div>
          <h3 id="cs-title">CardStock</h3>
          <p class="thesis">Designing honest interfaces for incomplete data.</p>
        </div>
        <dl class="case-meta">
          <dt>Role</dt><dd>Product, UX direction, sole developer</dd>
          <dt>Built with</dt><dd>.NET 10, Blazor WebAssembly, PostgreSQL</dd>
          <dt>Status</dt><dd>Live at <a href="https://cardstock.pro">cardstock.pro</a>, still in progress</dd>
        </dl>
      </div>
      <Shot Name="cs-screener" Width="1600" Height="1111" 
           Alt="CardStock screener backtest prototype: saved screens on the left; entry and exit rules; a plain-language summary that 11 buy signals were found, 75% of the 4 aged entries made money, and 7 entries have not aged 30 days yet; an equity curve against the market index; and result tiles with a return distribution."><Caption>Screener backtest, prototype. The result is written as a sentence, including what is not known yet.</Caption></Shot>
      <div class="states" role="list" aria-label="More CardStock screens">
        <Shot Name="cs-home" Width="1600" Height="1111" Role="listitem" Sizes="(max-width: 560px) 100vw, (max-width: 1200px) 33vw, 380px" 
           Alt="Home prototype: market strip, a feed of cards entering and leaving saved screens, a binder summary against the market index, and a watchlist with sparklines."><Caption>Home, prototype</Caption></Shot>
        <Shot Name="cs-card" Width="1600" Height="955" Role="listitem" Sizes="(max-width: 560px) 100vw, (max-width: 1200px) 33vw, 380px" 
           Alt="Card page prototype for Umbreon VMAX Alt Art: prices for six grade tiers, a signals panel showing 25 evaluated and 3 firing, and a 12-month price chart."><Caption>Card page, prototype</Caption></Shot>
        <Shot Name="cs-binder" Width="1600" Height="1000" Role="listitem" Sizes="(max-width: 560px) 100vw, (max-width: 1200px) 33vw, 380px" 
           Alt="Binder prototype: holdings with the screen each card came from, shown as Exited or Active badges with arrows, and EST badges on values no recent sale supports."><Caption>Binder, prototype. EST marks values no recent sale supports.</Caption></Shot>
      </div>
      <div class="case-body">
        <ul class="decisions">
          <li><h4>A state, not a fake number</h4><p>Each of the 30 indicators has a data-sufficiency floor. Below it, the screen shows a dash and says why, never a misleading figure.</p></li>
          <li><h4>Label every estimate</h4><p>Prices you enter never change. Current values move with the market, and any value without recent sales behind it carries an EST badge.</p></li>
          <li><h4>Say it in a sentence</h4><p>A backtest ends with plain words: how many signals, how many made money, and how many have not aged long enough to count.</p></li>
          <li><h4>Colour is never the only signal</h4><p>Gains carry signs, screen status carries a word and an arrow, and the app ships light, dark and two colourblind palettes.</p></li>
        </ul>
        <div class="rejected" role="note" aria-labelledby="rej-cs">
          <h4 id="rej-cs">Rejected: animating the jump</h4>
          <p>Validation messages made the transaction modal resize abruptly. Two fixes were built and compared live: animating the resize, or reserving space for the messages. I chose to reserve the space, so nothing moves at all.</p>
          <p>One of 310 entries in the CardStock decision ledger.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <h4 class="live-title">From the live app</h4>
      <p class="live-note">Sales data has been paused since mid-October while the Back Office replaces the blocked scraper, so the live charts are thin. They still show how the app handles what it has.</p>
      <Shot Name="cardstock-chart" Width="1600" Height="777" Class="live-shot" 
           Alt="Live CardStock chart page for Mega Charizard X EX: card art, PSA 10 price, grade tier chips, indicator values shown as dashes where data is too thin, and a price history with individual sales as dots and a dotted segment where no sales were recorded."><Caption>Indicators without enough data show dashes. Dotted segments mark days with no sales, instead of a line that pretends to know.</Caption></Shot>
    </article>

    <article class="case" id="iowa-bins" aria-labelledby="ib-title">
      <div class="case-head">
        <div>
          <h3 id="ib-title">Iowa Bins</h3>
          <p class="thesis">One service, two audiences: customers booking a move, and the crew running the day.</p>
        </div>
        <dl class="case-meta">
          <dt>Role</dt><dd>Product, UX direction, developer</dd>
          <dt>Built with</dt><dd>.NET 10 on a Raspberry Pi</dd>
          <dt>Scope</dt><dd>30 screens: booking, staff admin, emails, errors</dd>
          <dt>Status</dt><dd>Live at <a href="https://iowabins.com">iowabins.com</a>, still in progress</dd>
        </dl>
      </div>
      <div class="pair">
        <Shot Name="ib-booking" Width="1600" Height="1000" Sizes="(max-width: 560px) 100vw, (max-width: 1200px) 50vw, 576px" 
           Alt="Iowa Bins booking page prototype: tote count, rental length and delivery date on the left, with a running order total on the right that says nothing is charged today."><Caption>Customers: everything on one page, with the total always in view</Caption></Shot>
        <Shot Name="ib-dispatch" Width="1600" Height="1111" Sizes="(max-width: 560px) 100vw, (max-width: 1200px) 50vw, 576px" 
           Alt="Iowa Bins staff dispatch prototype: morning and afternoon stops in order, a capacity meter near its limit, yard inventory, and a failed-charge flag on one stop."><Caption>Staff: the day's run, capacity and yard inventory</Caption></Shot>
      </div>
      <div class="states" role="list" aria-label="Iowa Bins edge screens">
        <Shot Name="ib-confirmed" Width="1600" Height="1000" Role="listitem" Sizes="(max-width: 560px) 100vw, (max-width: 1200px) 33vw, 380px" 
           Alt="Order confirmed prototype: headline 'You're on the schedule', a note that nothing is charged today, and the order total."><Caption>Confirmation: when the card is charged, in plain words</Caption></Shot>
        <Shot Name="ib-emails" Width="1600" Height="955" Role="listitem" Sizes="(max-width: 560px) 100vw, (max-width: 1200px) 33vw, 380px" 
           Alt="Emails and texts spec: twenty messages listed, with the booking confirmation's trigger, subject, preheader, text version and rendered email."><Caption>Every message the system sends, specified</Caption></Shot>
        <Shot Name="ib-404" Width="1600" Height="1000" Role="listitem" Sizes="(max-width: 560px) 100vw, (max-width: 1200px) 33vw, 380px" 
           Alt="404 page prototype: headline 'This one is not on the truck' with links to reserve totes, the estimator, pricing, what fits in a tote, how it works and the FAQ."><Caption>404: a way forward, not a dead end</Caption></Shot>
      </div>
      <div class="case-body">
        <ul class="decisions">
          <li><h4>Say when money moves</h4><p>"Nothing is charged until the morning of delivery" sits beside the total, because that is the question a customer has before they book.</p></li>
          <li><h4>One owner for every change</h4><p>Customers never edit an order themselves; they call or text and staff make the change. Every screen, email and FAQ answer was rewritten to agree.</p></li>
          <li><h4>Design the edges</h4><p>Twenty emails and texts are specified with their triggers, and error pages send people somewhere useful.</p></li>
          <li><h4>Audit the whole set</h4><p>A sweep of all 30 screens found places where they contradicted each other. I settled each one and sent a single fix list back.</p></li>
        </ul>
        <div class="rejected" role="note" aria-labelledby="rej-ib">
          <h4 id="rej-ib">Rejected: self-service changes</h4>
          <p>Letting customers move or cancel from My Order meant instant charges and edge cases a small crew could not support. A read-only order page and a phone number were kinder.</p>
          <p>From the owner decisions log.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </article>

    <div class="minor">
      <article id="back-office" aria-labelledby="bo-title">
        <h3 id="bo-title">CardStock Back Office</h3>
        <p class="thesis">Rebuilding a data pipeline after the source shut the door.</p>
        <p>When PriceCharting blocked the scraper that fed CardStock, I didn't work around the block. I researched replacements on a $100-a-month budget, asked sources for permission, and wrote a design spec before any code. The new .NET worker keeps a catalogue linked across three sources and collects eBay and TCGplayer sales through a paid sales-data API.</p>
        <ul class="facts-list">
          <li><b>481 commits and 866 tests</b></li>
          <li>Sources never overwrite each other. A nightly cross-check turns every disagreement into an open issue.</li>
          <li>Built on a staging Pi while production keeps running. CI refuses a deploy when a test fails.</li>
          <li>One daily report email, and no routine hand work</li>
        </ul>
        <dl class="case-meta">
          <dt>Built with</dt><dd>.NET 10 worker, PostgreSQL, GitHub Actions</dd>
          <dt>Status</dt><dd>In staging, cutover pending</dd>
        </dl>
      </article>
      <article id="cipher" aria-labelledby="ci-title">
        <h3 id="ci-title">Cipher cracker</h3>
        <p class="thesis">Teaching myself cryptanalysis on a cipher I haven't cracked yet.</p>
        <p>A 29,411-digit ciphertext, digits only, believed to hide English. I used it to learn classical cryptanalysis from the ground up: frequency and n-gram analysis, index of coincidence, Kasiski examination, entropy, and simulated annealing for key search. Two .NET 10 apps ran on Raspberry Pi 5s for days at a time, with Claude proposing strategies and me running, checking and steering them.</p>
        <ul class="facts-list">
          <li><b>8 hypotheses refuted</b> and 2 still open, each with the evidence that decided it</li>
          <li><b>222.5 billion</b> straddling-checkerboard candidates tested. None read as English.</li>
          <li>32 hours of simulated annealing ruled out every Gronsfeld key from length 2 to 20.</li>
          <li>Digit-pair substitution hit a hard ceiling across 288 billion candidates, which ruled out a fixed pair code.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="learned"><strong>What it taught me:</strong> proving what a cipher isn't takes the same rigour as proving what it is.</p>
        <dl class="case-meta">
          <dt>Built with</dt><dd>.NET 10, two Raspberry Pi 5s</dd>
          <dt>Status</dt><dd>Paused</dd>
        </dl>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
